@meta
id: 28-10
title: Choosing a leader key
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-mappings
par: 136
minutes: 5
vscode: chords like Ctrl-K Ctrl-S, which you also had to choose once
open: .vimrc

@brief
Every key in normal mode already does something, so your own mappings
need a prefix that does not. That prefix is the leader: press it, then
press a letter, and vim runs your command. `<leader>` in a mapping stands
for whatever you set `mapleader` to.

The default is backslash, which is awkward on most keyboards. The two
common replacements are comma — close to the fingers, but it is the
"repeat `f` backwards" key, which you will miss — and Space, which does
nothing in normal mode that `l` does not do better. Space is the usual
answer, and it is the one to take unless you have a reason.

Set it at the top of the Mappings section, with the reason above it, and
add the first mapping that uses it: `<leader>w` writes the file.

    " space: big, central, and means nothing on its own
    let mapleader = " "
    nnoremap <C-l> :nohlsearch<CR><C-l>
    nnoremap Y y$
    nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR>

The order matters more than it looks. `<leader>` is expanded when the
mapping is *defined*, not when you press it, so a mapping written above
the `let` line quietly binds to backslash instead, forever.

@keys
| `let mapleader = " "` | make Space the leader |
| `O` | open a line above |
| `<CR>` | how a mapping spells Enter |

@hints
`let mapleader` has to sit above every mapping that uses `<leader>`, so
it goes at the top of the section — above the two mappings already there.
--
Search for `nohlsearch` to land on the first mapping, then `O` to open a
line above it. Once you are on the `let` line, a second `O` gives you the
comment line without vim adding a `"` of its own.
--
Then `G` and `o` for the new `<leader>w` mapping at the bottom. `:w<CR>`
is typed literally, angle brackets and all.

@solution
    /nohlsearch<Enter> O let mapleader = " " <Esc>
    O " space: big, central, and means nothing on its own <Esc>
    G o nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR> <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Three things about leaders that save arguments later.

`mapleader` is read at definition time. Change it halfway down your
config and everything above keeps the old prefix, which is the single
most confusing thing a vimrc can do to you. Set it once, at the top,
before any mapping.

There is a second one, `maplocalleader`, for mappings that only apply to
one kind of file — the per-filetype files in lesson 28-13 are where those
live. Plugins use it too. Backslash-comma or a plain comma is the usual
choice; you can ignore it until a plugin asks.

And keep the leader mappings mnemonic and few. `<leader>w` write,
`<leader>h` clear the highlight, `<leader>b` buffers. If you cannot
remember what `<leader>gq` does, you will never press it, and it is
costing you a line of config and a moment of doubt every time you read
the file.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Mappings ---
nnoremap <C-l> :nohlsearch<CR><C-l>
nnoremap Y y$

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Mappings ---
" space: big, central, and means nothing on its own
let mapleader = " "
nnoremap <C-l> :nohlsearch<CR><C-l>
nnoremap Y y$
nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR>
